## Your slots

1. **pillars** — {{ skeleton.min_chapters }}..{{ skeleton.max_chapters }} chapter headings, cited from heading lines
   (h1/h2/h3): the things the site itself leads with. Each becomes a
   chapter; their ORDER is your narrative.
2. **hero** — `hero_word`: the product's name as the site writes it (cite a
   title/h1 line); `hero_sub`: the site's tagline (cite).
3. **cold_open** — 1..3 short hook lines, FREE TEXT (your creative channel):
   at most 3 words each (CJK: at most 8 characters). A hook is the film's
   own framing — rhythm, a question, a tease. It is NEVER a product claim:
   no features, numbers, customers or promises the page does not state —
   anything factual must be cited, and this slot cannot cite.
4. **captions** — 0..6 walk captions, each `{"scene": "<a ui scene key>",
   "text": REF}` citing a line FROM THE PAGE THAT SCENE SHOWS; at most 2
   per scene. A caption names what the camera is pushing in on.
5. **reveal** — the "one more thing" beat: `reveal_lead` is FREE TEXT (null
   = the system's standard lead-in); `reveal_word` (1-2 words) and
   `reveal_sub` (one line) are cited — something true the site states but
   does not lead with (a free tier, a number, an integration).
6. **end** — `end_name` (the product's name) and `end_tagline`, both cited.
7. **narration** — spoken voiceover, keyed by scene key: one or two natural
   sentences per scene, ≤ 20 chars per second. Speech is your creative
   channel too: rephrase the page's facts into natural spoken language —
   TRUE to the manifest (never invent a claim), in the FILM's language (the
   pinned one above, else the PAGE's own — never the conversation's). Skip
   scenes that play better silent (a divider beat, the fast cold open);
   narrate the scenes where speech carries the story (hero, walks, reveal,
   end).
8. **name** — a short display name for the film (null if unsure); **theme** —
   canvas/ink hex pair from the site's look (null = default).

## Citations (binding for every factual slot)

- A factual on-screen string is a REFERENCE, never a composition:
  `{"line": 7}` renders line L7 verbatim. You choose WHICH line carries
  each beat; the system renders its exact words. A factual string you typed
  yourself is wrong by construction — the resolver only accepts ids.
- Over-long lines: when the line you want exceeds its slot's character cap,
  set `"fit": "head"` (keep the leading segment) or `"fit": "tail"` (keep
  the trailing one) — the system trims at a word boundary. With `"fit"`
  omitted ("full") the line must fit the cap as-is, so cite a shorter line
  instead of forcing a long one.
- Accent: `"accent": "<substring>"` wraps one substring of the RESOLVED
  text in emphasis marks. The substring must exist in the resolved text —
  a trim that cuts your accent away is a citation error.
- If your previous direction was rejected: a rejection names the slot and
  the cause (unknown line id, missed accent, caption from the wrong page,
  resolved text over its cap, a creative slot blank or over-long). Fix THE
  SLOT — never answer a rejection by writing factual strings.

## Output (JSON, no prose)

{
  "name": string | null,
  "theme": {"canvas": "#RRGGBB", "ink": "#RRGGBB"} | null,
  "language": string,        // the film's spoken language (ISO code) — the pinned
                             // value when one is given above, else the page's own
  "pillars": [REF],          // 3..5 cited heading lines
  "hero_word": REF,          // resolves to ≤16 chars
  "hero_sub": REF,           // ≤90
  "cold_open": [string],     // 1..3 free-text hooks, each ≤30 chars (≤3 words; CJK ≤8 chars)
  "captions": [{"scene": string, "text": REF}],   // 0..6, each resolves to ≤60 chars
  "reveal_lead": string | null,  // free text, ≤60 chars; null = the system's lead-in
  "reveal_word": REF,        // ≤24
  "reveal_sub": REF,         // ≤90
  "end_name": REF,           // ≤24
  "end_tagline": REF,        // ≤90
  "narrations": {"<scene key>": string}  // only the scenes you narrate
}

`REF` = `{"line": int, "fit": "full"|"head"|"tail", "accent": string | null}`
(`fit`/`accent` optional).

